# project-nordly — service architecture standard

All services target the same layered shape, modelled on the reference service
`search-performance`. New services start from `services/template/`.

## Layering

```
client → internal/app/api/<name>/        transport: 1 RPC = 1 file, proto<->domain mapping, error mapping
       → internal/<domain>/service/        thin orchestrator: Service interface, wires usecase handlers
       → internal/<domain>/usecase/command/<op>/   write op:  command.go (Command+Validate) + handler.go (New+Handle) + mocks/
         internal/<domain>/usecase/query/<op>/     read op:   query.go   (Query+Validate)   + handler.go (New+Handle) + mocks/
       → internal/<domain>/repository/      concrete Postgres/Redis behind a Store/port interface
       | internal/adapter/<vendor>/         external gRPC/HTTP clients (one per vendor) + mocks/
```

Per-domain folders: `model/` (+ `errors.go` sentinels), `repository/` (+ `store.go` port),
`service/`, `usecase/{command,query}/`. Add `dto/`, `enum/`, `converter/` when mapping
or typing grows. Cross-cutting: `internal/config/`, `internal/tools/`, background workers
under `internal/<worker>/` or `internal/services/`.

## Reference exemplars (copy these shapes)

- Query: `services/template/internal/example/usecase/query/get_item/`
- Command: `services/interview/internal/interview/usecase/command/submit_attempt/`
- Repository port: `services/notes/internal/notes/repository/store.go`

## Domain package layout (search-performance shape)

```
internal/<domain>/
├── model/          # entities + errors.go sentinels (+ enums.go when needed)
├── dto/            # optional — DB/API transfer shapes that are not domain entities
├── enum/           # optional — typed closed sets (or model/enums.go)
├── requests/       # optional — service-facing request/response DTOs
├── repository/     # store.go (port) + concrete Postgres/Redis + mocks/
├── service/        # Service interface + thin orchestrator (wires usecase handlers)
│   └── <op>.go     # one public method per file when the surface grows
├── usecase/
│   ├── command/<op>/   # command.go + handler.go (+ *_test.go) + mocks/
│   └── query/<op>/     # query.go   + handler.go (+ *_test.go) + mocks/
└── …

internal/adapter/<vendor>/   # one package per external system; adapter.go + method files + mocks/
internal/app/api/<name>/     # transport: service.go + one RPC per file + mapper/errors/register/gateway
```

External reference: Ozon `search-performance` (full mature CQRS service).
Nordly exemplars: `services/template` (query), `services/notes` (commands + Store port).

## File declaration order (within a `.go` file)

Match search-performance / Uber-style readability — **top → bottom**:

1. `//go:generate` mockery lines (above or immediately after `package`)
2. `package`
3. `import`
4. `const` / `var` (package-level)
5. **Interfaces** (consumer-side ports for *this* package)
6. **Config / Deps** struct (constructor input)
7. **Handler / Adapter / Implementation / concrete** struct
8. **`New` / `NewHandler` / `NewAdapter`** constructor
9. **Exported methods** (`Handle`, public API) — one concern; keep helpers below
10. **Unexported helpers** last

| Kind | Files | Order inside |
|------|-------|--------------|
| Usecase command | `command.go` → `Command` + `Validate()` (+ small helpers) | then `handler.go` → ports → Config → Handler → New → Handle → private |
| Usecase query | `query.go` → `Query` + `Validate()` | then `handler.go` same as command |
| Domain service | `service.go` → `Service` interface → concrete struct → `Deps` → `New` | one method file per op when large |
| Transport | `service.go` → domain `Service` iface → `Implementation` → `New` | one RPC file = thin map → call → map error |
| Adapter | `adapter.go` → ports → `Adapter` → `New` | one method file per remote call |
| Repository | `store.go` → `Store` port + `var _ Store = (*Repository)(nil)` | concrete methods in other files |

## Conventions

### Consumer-side interfaces

Declare the **narrow** interface a handler/service needs in *that* package. The concrete
repo/adapter satisfies it. Never import `*repository.Repository` from usecase/service.

```go
// DO — in usecase/command/foo/handler.go
type Store interface {
    GetNote(ctx context.Context, id string) (*model.Note, error)
    SaveNote(ctx context.Context, n *model.Note) error
}

// DON'T — depend on the whole concrete type
func New(repo *repository.Repository) *Handler // too wide; couples to Postgres
```

### Mocks — mockery only

```go
//go:generate go run github.com/vektra/mockery/v2@v2.53.5 --case=underscore --with-expecter --name=Store
```

- Run `go generate ./...` after changing a port interface.
- **Never** hand-write `fakeRepo` / `stubBilling` / `mockStore` structs in `*_test.go`.
- Use generated `mocks/` + expecters only.
- Exception: thin `pgx.Row` / `pgx.Rows` driver stubs when testing SQL scanning.
- When editing a package that still has handmade fakes, rewrite those tests to mockery
  **in the same change set**.

```go
// DO
store := mocks.NewStore(t)
store.EXPECT().GetNote(mock.Anything, "id").Return(note, nil)

// DON'T
type fakeStore struct{ note *model.Note }
func (f *fakeStore) GetNote(...) (*model.Note, error) { return f.note, nil }
```

### Prefer `slices` / `sort`

| Instead of | Use |
|------------|-----|
| manual `for` + `append` clone | `slices.Clone(s)` |
| membership `for` loop | `slices.Contains` / `ContainsFunc` |
| find index loop | `slices.Index` / `IndexFunc` |
| filter-in-place loop | `slices.DeleteFunc` |
| `sort.Slice(s, func(i,j int) bool { return s[i].X < s[j].X })` | `slices.SortFunc` / `SortStableFunc` |

Do **not** replace a clear domain loop that does more than clone/filter/sort with a
forced `slices` call — idioms first, cleverness never.

### Fail at construction; delete dead branches

- Required deps: reject in `config.Load()`, `cmd/*/app/run.go`, or `New(Deps)` /
  `NewHandler(Config)` by returning an error. **No** `if deps.X == nil { return Err… }`
  mid-`Handle`.
- After `Validate()` / constructor invariants, do not re-check the same conditions
  “just in case”.
- If a branch cannot run given prior validation, **delete it**.

```go
// DO
func New(deps Deps) (*Handler, error) {
    if deps.Store == nil {
        return nil, errors.New("foo.Handler: Store is required")
    }
    return &Handler{store: deps.Store}, nil
}

// DON'T — mid-request nil guard on a wired dep
func (h *Handler) Handle(ctx context.Context, cmd Command) error {
    if h.store == nil {
        return errors.New("store not configured")
    }
    ...
}
```

### No unused fields; no redundant SQL

- Remove model/DTO/struct fields that nothing reads or maps to proto/DB.
- Do not `SELECT` columns the caller never uses; do not run a second query for data
  already loaded in the same flow.
- Delete dead RPCs/proto fields at the root (proto → gen → transport → service → repo),
  verified against clients — see fail-fast rule.

### Transport, validation, errors

- **Transport mappers**: return an error on nil required entities — never
  return empty proto as success.
- **Validation**: each `Command`/`Query` has `Validate()`; missing/bad input wraps a
  sentinel (`ErrInvalidArgument`/`ErrInvalidInput`) → gRPC `InvalidArgument`.
- **Errors**: wrap with `%w`; domain sentinels in `model/errors.go` (shared by service +
  usecase); transport `mapServiceError` maps them to codes.
- **Transactions**: multi-write → repository `WithTx(ctx, fn)` + `conn(ctx)`;
  idempotency (outbox/processed_events) inside the tx.
- **Quotas / side effects**: gate before work; consume after durable write; compensate
  on later failure.
- **One RPC per transport file**; thin handlers (validate → call service → map error).
- **Typed enums, not raw strings**: proto `enum` + `model/enums.go` snake_case constants
  + `proto_enums.go` mapping. No bare `string` for closed sets in public API/domain.
- English code/logs/comments; user commits manually; minimal diffs.

## DO / DON'T (quick reference)

| DO | DON'T |
|----|-------|
| Narrow consumer-side port in handler package | Depend on `*repository.Repository` from usecase |
| `//go:generate mockery` + `mocks.NewX(t)` expecters | Hand-write `fakeRepo` / `stubX` in tests |
| `slices.Contains` / `SortFunc` / `Clone` / `DeleteFunc` | Manual membership/sort/clone boilerplate |
| Return error from `New`/`Load` on missing required dep | `if s.repo == nil` inside `Handle` |
| Delete unreachable branches after `Validate()` | Keep “defensive” invent-default arms |
| Drop unused struct fields and unused `SELECT`s | Leave dead DTO fields “for later” |
| Extract complex writes into `usecase/command/<op>/` | Big-bang rewrite of whole service |
| Keep trivial CRUD reads inline in service | Over-engineer every Get into a query package |

## Migration posture

Don't big-bang. Per service: fix correctness first, then add repository ports + mocks
+ tests, then extract the highest-complexity operations into `usecase/` packages.
Keep trivial CRUD reads inline — don't over-engineer.

## Build / verify (per service, from its root)

```bash
cd services/<name>
GOPROXY=https://proxy.golang.org,direct GOWORK=off go build ./...
GOPROXY=https://proxy.golang.org,direct GOWORK=off go test ./...
GOWORK=off govulncheck ./...   # CI gate — bump Fixed-in versions across CI_SERVICES
```

Govulncheck failures: see [.cursor/rules/go-backend.mdc](go-backend.mdc) (bump `grpc` / `x/text` / … in every affected service, re-scan).

